00:19:20.080 --> 00:19:21.140
And then the first

529
00:19:21.140 --> 00:19:22.900
night we're like, yeah, Booze Cruise.

530
00:19:23.140 --> 00:19:24.040
We're still, we're doing it.

531
00:19:24.140 --> 00:19:24.480
We're doing it.

532
00:19:24.560 --> 00:19:25.680
Like by the third

533
00:19:25.680 --> 00:19:30.480
night, no one will speak to me, but you definitely see the progression.

534
00:19:30.840 --> 00:19:33.780
I watched the whole thing and it just made me smile.

535
00:19:34.040 --> 00:19:36.580
I just, man, it just brought me right back.

536
00:19:36.760 --> 00:19:38.500
And we also look 12 years old.

537
00:19:38.700 --> 00:19:39.400
We do.

538
00:19:39.680 --> 00:19:44.560
We just look so, I was like, oh man, time has marched on across my face.

539
00:19:44.820 --> 00:19:45.680
Oh no.

540
00:19:46.400 --> 00:19:48.220
I was like, wow, we look so young.

541
00:19:48.600 --> 00:19:49.860
Well, that's all I've got.

542
00:19:50.380 --> 00:19:53.460
Should we go get Greg and bring him in here and break down this episode?

543
00:19:53.460 --> 00:19:53.880
Yes.

544
00:19:54.000 --> 00:19:56.200
Cause I have a ton of note cards.

545
00:19:56.520 --> 00:19:57.420
You really do.

546
00:19:57.600 --> 00:19:58.200
I really do.

547
00:19:58.400 --> 00:20:00.920
Angela guys got here early because.

548
00:20:01.060 --> 00:20:02.440
Because I had so many note cards to lay out.

549
00:20:02.440 --> 00:20:06.500
She had so many note cards that she was like, I came in and I was shocked.

550
00:20:06.660 --> 00:20:08.180
I said, what is this?

551
00:20:08.660 --> 00:20:09.560
Here it is though.

552
00:20:09.720 --> 00:20:10.780
All right, well, let's do it.

553
00:20:19.910 --> 00:20:24.370
Okay guys, we're back from our break and we are sitting here with Greg Daniels.

554
00:20:24.530 --> 00:20:24.950
Yay.

555
00:20:25.350 --> 00:20:25.400
Yay.

556
00:20:25.750 --> 00:20:28.410
Writer of Booze Cruise, showrunner of The Office.

557
00:20:28.930 --> 00:20:30.950
Greg, thank you so much for coming in.

558
00:20:30.950 --> 00:20:32.530
Well, thank you so much for inviting me.

559
00:20:32.710 --> 00:20:33.690
I love your show.

560
00:20:33.810 --> 00:20:34.990
You guys are so good together.

561
00:20:35.270 --> 00:20:41.510
It's so entertaining to hear you and it's so fun for me to hear your voices and your points of view and everything.

562
00:20:41.970 --> 00:20:43.090
Thank you so much, Greg.

563
00:20:43.090 --> 00:20:46.450
If not for you, Greg, we would not be best friends and this show wouldn't exiSt.

564
00:20:46.890 --> 00:20:53.670
Our lives would be completely, I don't even know, I can't even put it into words, honestly, cause now I'll get emotional.

565
00:20:53.870 --> 00:20:54.230
Here I go.

566
00:20:54.310 --> 00:20:55.270
Angela's crying already.

567
00:20:55.910 --> 00:20:57.090
I can't, sorry.

568
00:20:57.370 --> 00:20:58.690
It just all kind of hit me.

569
00:20:58.690 --> 00:21:07.090
Do you know how Jim says he'll bet a paycheck that Michael in this episode is going to do the Titanic thing?

570
00:21:07.270 --> 00:21:07.530
Yeah.

571
00:21:07.710 --> 00:21:10.070
I would have said Angela was going to tear up.

572
00:21:10.470 --> 00:21:10.950
But I...

573
00:21:11.710 --> 00:21:12.890
And it happened already.

574
00:21:13.130 --> 00:21:13.870
It happened so soon.

575
00:21:13.910 --> 00:21:14.950
I know, I'm sorry.

576
00:21:15.210 --> 00:21:20.210
I just, it just all, like the wave hit me of just gratitude I have for you.

577
00:21:20.370 --> 00:21:22.170
Just absolute gratitude in my heart.

578
00:21:22.170 --> 00:21:22.650
Likewise.

579
00:21:22.930 --> 00:21:25.450
It was a great, it was a great time for all of us.

580
00:21:25.570 --> 00:21:30.030
And if your friendship was the only thing that came out of it, that would be cool.

581
00:21:30.330 --> 00:21:30.650
Oh.

582
00:21:30.850 --> 00:21:32.070
And yet it's not, is it?

583
00:21:32.330 --> 00:21:32.380
I know.

584
00:21:34.490 --> 00:21:40.910
Well, Greg, when we have guests, we always like to start with asking them, how did they end up being on The Office?

585
00:21:41.310 --> 00:21:44.090
How did you end up developing The Office for American Television?

586
00:21:44.090 --> 00:21:52.730
I will go back a little ways because I was kind of already with a career at the time that the show started.

587
00:21:52.970 --> 00:21:54.910
You had a very successful career, Greg.

588
00:21:55.410 --> 00:21:56.530
I mean, you had done King of the...

589
00:21:56.530 --> 00:21:59.550
I won't list your IMDb, but you were the real deal.

590
00:21:59.830 --> 00:22:00.310
Go ahead.

591
00:22:00.890 --> 00:22:01.950
People don't know it enough.

592
00:22:02.370 --> 00:22:03.830
You created King of the Hill.

593
00:22:03.910 --> 00:22:04.330
King of the Hill.

594
00:22:04.350 --> 00:22:05.330
And you were running that show.

595
00:22:05.590 --> 00:22:06.050
Yeah, yeah.

596
00:22:06.050 --> 00:22:08.650
But you had written on Saturday Night Live and...

597
00:22:08.650 --> 00:22:09.050
Simpsons.

598
00:22:09.050 --> 00:22:10.270
The Simpsons forever.

599
00:22:10.830 --> 00:22:14.370
And oh my gosh, there's that one song that you wrote on The Simpsons that is just amazing.

600
00:22:14.790 --> 00:22:15.670
Anyway, okay, okay.

601
00:22:15.670 --> 00:22:16.510
Who needs a quickie mart?

602
00:22:16.670 --> 00:22:17.530
Yes, who needs?

603
00:22:17.570 --> 00:22:17.970
Yes!

604
00:22:18.210 --> 00:22:18.490
Yes!

605
00:22:19.150 --> 00:22:21.130
Okay, so you were the real deal.

606
00:22:21.610 --> 00:22:22.530
Things were cooking.

607
00:22:22.830 --> 00:22:24.170
So yeah, so I was in a good position.

608
00:22:24.170 --> 00:22:29.530
You know how they say like you need 10,000 hours to be good at something?

609
00:22:29.730 --> 00:22:30.010
Yes.

610
00:22:30.150 --> 00:22:36.910
So I had sort of 10,000 hours on The Simpsons and Saturday Night Live, and then I had another like 15,000 hours on King of the Hill.

611
00:22:37.030 --> 00:22:38.610
So I was like prepared.

612
00:22:39.330 --> 00:22:52.110
And what happened was my agent sent me the tape of the office, and it was pretty unknown over here, and it had a pretty boring title, and I didn't watch it.

613
00:22:52.810 --> 00:22:53.930
The BBC version.

614
00:22:54.050 --> 00:22:55.050
The BBC version, yes.

615
00:22:55.290 --> 00:22:56.790
And so he called me.

616
00:22:56.890 --> 00:23:00.730
It was over Christmas, like two years before our show came out.

617
00:23:01.490 --> 00:23:04.650
And he said, all right, I'm going to send this to somebody else if you don't watch it.

618
00:23:04.710 --> 00:23:06.790
And I was like, oh, okay, hang on, hang on, I'll watch it tonight.

619
00:23:07.250 --> 00:23:12.510
And I put it in around 11, and I stayed up till like 3 in the morning watching it, and I thought it was just brilliant.

620
00:23:14.490 --> 00:23:18.070
So, you know, I called and I said, I really want to meet these guys.

621
00:23:18.070 --> 00:23:28.950
And I had been kind of like a good little student, you know, always in my career, and I always wanted to interact with people who I respected.

622
00:23:29.230 --> 00:23:37.270
And, you know, I sold an episode to Seinfeld so I could work with Larry David, and that was like a career important move for me.

623
00:23:37.270 --> 00:23:45.950
And I kind of identified that Ricky Gervais and Stephen Merchant had done something that put them in the top ranks of people working in this profession.

624
00:23:45.950 --> 00:23:47.250
So I really wanted to meet them.

625
00:23:47.350 --> 00:23:51.750
And I didn't really think it could be adapted, so I was kind of scamming them.

626
00:23:51.870 --> 00:23:54.530
I was like, I'm going to go meet you and take this interesting meeting.

627
00:23:54.530 --> 00:23:56.150
Take your knowledge from you.

628
00:23:56.310 --> 00:23:56.870
Ask you questions.

629
00:23:56.890 --> 00:23:59.690
Build a relationship, maybe for something in the future.

630
00:23:59.690 --> 00:24:00.210
You never know.

631
00:24:00.290 --> 00:24:01.070
It's not going to be this.

632
00:24:01.070 --> 00:24:12.210
And anyway, so we met over at Ben Silverman's office because he had the rights, and we got along very well because it turned out that they loved American TV,

633
00:24:12.430 --> 00:24:20.370
and specifically The Simpsons, and specifically their favorite episode or Ricky's favorite episode was Homer Badman, which I had written.

634
00:24:20.550 --> 00:24:22.070
And so we kind of hit it off.

635
00:24:22.070 --> 00:24:22.710
That's amazing.

636
00:24:22.710 --> 00:24:22.950
Yeah.

637
00:24:23.230 --> 00:24:36.030
And I was telling them, you know, all the kind of theories that I had about making a TV show that I put into place in King of the Hill, which was, you know,

638
00:24:36.230 --> 00:24:44.030
a slower pace and allowing for awkwardness and realism and all this stuff, and they really responded to that.

639
00:24:46.290 --> 00:25:00.150
So we decided, well, maybe I would be the person to adapt it into America, and then there was a couple of, you know, months where we were kind of hammering it out,

640
00:25:00.270 --> 00:25:10.190
but it was always, the thought was always to bring it to someplace cool and cable-y because the show was not like what was on TV.

641
00:25:10.670 --> 00:25:17.030
You know, I think the biggest show at the time was Will and Grace, which was very kind of multi-camera, fast-paced.

642
00:25:17.030 --> 00:25:17.850
Far more broad.

643
00:25:18.030 --> 00:25:19.930
Yeah, very different feel, you know, sort of classic.

644
00:25:19.930 --> 00:25:22.470
Yeah, it didn't have a network feel of, like, television.

645
00:25:22.590 --> 00:25:23.790
Yeah, it felt weird.

646
00:25:23.870 --> 00:25:31.530
And so we were talking about, well, maybe we'll go to HBO, and apparently Kevin Reilly at FX really likes it.

647
00:25:32.350 --> 00:25:34.210
And so we had a couple of thoughts.

648
00:25:34.410 --> 00:25:39.790
And anyway, so I signed on to do it, and then Kevin Reilly left FX and went to NBC.

649
00:25:42.390 --> 00:25:45.150
And HBO didn't want to do it because they didn't want to do a remake.

650
00:25:47.130 --> 00:25:49.550
And Ben was like, you've got to sell this to NBC.

651
00:25:50.150 --> 00:25:55.370
And then I got very worried because I was like, this is not going to work on NBC, you know?

652
00:25:55.570 --> 00:25:57.350
You're like, this isn't what I signed up for.

653
00:25:57.550 --> 00:25:57.610
Yeah.

654
00:25:57.850 --> 00:25:58.870
Cool cable show.

655
00:25:58.990 --> 00:25:59.670
Yeah, exactly.

656
00:25:59.670 --> 00:26:12.150
And I also had these stress dreams where I would be put on trial by all the comedy nerds in the world for doing this lousy NBC version of The Office, you know?

657
00:26:12.410 --> 00:26:13.050
Yeah.

658
00:26:13.050 --> 00:26:24.110
Yeah, so – but eventually we were like, well, maybe the value of it will be to move network comedy into a new direction,

659
00:26:24.530 --> 00:26:35.430
maybe open it up just a little bit and make it more like what I liked about animation, which was single camera and didn't have a laugh track and stuff like that.

660
00:26:35.430 --> 00:26:40.010
So I was like, okay, I kind of convinced myself it was worth doing just for that.

661
00:26:40.250 --> 00:26:42.890
And it ushered in this whole new type of American television.

662
00:26:43.490 --> 00:26:46.710
All right, well, let's get into Booze Cruise.

663
00:26:47.110 --> 00:26:48.150
All right, let's do it, Jenna.

664
00:26:48.550 --> 00:26:51.130
What was the inspiration for this episode?

665
00:26:51.130 --> 00:27:01.710
You know, one of the things we were doing was we were looking for classic office situations and trying to test them for – was there comic potential?

666
00:27:01.930 --> 00:27:07.450
And the idea of going on a Booze Cruise sounded funny.

667
00:27:07.590 --> 00:27:10.690
And this was like our biggest episode yet, you know?

668
00:27:10.770 --> 00:27:15.150
This was where we were kind of feeling some oats as a show.

669
00:27:15.330 --> 00:27:18.850
And if you remember, they bought a whole bunch of hotel rooMs.

670
00:27:19.050 --> 00:27:19.250
Yeah.

671
00:27:20.370 --> 00:27:24.150
And this was sort of going to be like the new thing.

672
00:27:24.290 --> 00:27:28.850
We were maybe going to have more location shooting, and it was very exciting.

673
00:27:29.310 --> 00:27:39.510
Speaking of the Booze Cruise, when we were on the boat, I remember I was standing next to you, and I had told you that I had been on a Booze Cruise, and I had done a snorkel shot,

674
00:27:39.510 --> 00:27:41.870
like when we were talking about that.

675
00:27:42.110 --> 00:27:44.090
Did we roll with that right away from that?

676
00:27:44.290 --> 00:27:44.750
No, no, no.

677
00:27:44.750 --> 00:27:51.110
Well, we were – so I think we read – we had the table read, we read the script, and I went up to you, and I was like, this is great.

678
00:27:51.110 --> 00:27:54.790
I said, you know, I went on one of these, and it is – it gets crazy.

679
00:27:54.970 --> 00:28:00.810
I mean, there were snorkel shots, and you're like, snorkel shots, and you're like, okay, I want to talk to you more about snorkel shots.

680
00:28:00.810 --> 00:28:05.410
And then you kind of went off, and we were like – just like – you were busy with the episode.

681
00:28:05.410 --> 00:28:11.210
It was a very big episode, and then we were on the boat, and I think you had mentioned it to Phil Shea, and so he had snorkels.

682
00:28:11.370 --> 00:28:16.150
And I just remember all of a sudden I was like talking to Brian and Oscar, and I heard, Angela, Angela, get over here.

683
00:28:16.210 --> 00:28:17.490
And it was you, and you're like, get over here.

684
00:28:17.890 --> 00:28:19.350
Talk to them about snorkel shots.

685
00:28:19.530 --> 00:28:21.190
I was like, oh, okay.

686
00:28:21.430 --> 00:28:28.150
You poured it at the top, but you know, and so we sort of had this quick snorkel shot tutorial, and then it made it in the episode.

687
00:28:30.810 --> 00:28:39.490
I always – we talk about this a lot, how you were so open to like in the moment, like collaboration and getting people's ideas, and it made for such a fun,

688
00:28:39.750 --> 00:28:40.870
awesome work environment.

689
00:28:41.170 --> 00:28:42.110
Yes, completely.

690
00:28:42.410 --> 00:28:44.830
I love the work that you guys did because, you know,

691
00:28:45.030 --> 00:28:56.590
part of the thing that I wanted to do with the show was like the experiences that I'd had that I didn't like in show business were mostly like factory-made TV

692
00:28:56.590 --> 00:29:07.750
where they keep the writers over here, and the actors are over here, and nobody talks to each other, and they got to get the scripts out fast, and it didn't feel very creative.

693
00:29:07.750 --> 00:29:21.150
I really wanted to mix it all and have a more handmade feel to it, and so I had a lot of the writers act, and I would ask some of the actors to write,

694
00:29:21.270 --> 00:29:27.110
and I always love to get access to the kind of actor-prepares work that you guys did.

695
00:29:27.110 --> 00:29:36.330
And, you know, and I would hop on any great ad-lib, like that whole thing with Rain being Amish just came out of an ad-lib, if you remember,

696
00:29:36.610 --> 00:29:41.390
where he was talking about his grandfather's name, and he said, Dwight Shrewd, Amish.

697
00:29:41.590 --> 00:29:42.750
And then we were like, ooh.

698
00:29:43.110 --> 00:29:44.490
And then you just ran with it.

699
00:29:44.630 --> 00:29:45.810
Yeah, that sounded fun.

700
00:29:45.810 --> 00:29:58.790
And I also remember in the casting process, like I, you know, we initially wanted Steve, and then he took that other show, Come to Papa, before Allison Jones came on board.

701
00:29:58.950 --> 00:29:59.990
I don't know if you know that.

702
00:30:00.070 --> 00:30:00.290
No.

703
00:30:00.610 --> 00:30:00.790
Yeah.

704
00:30:00.990 --> 00:30:07.350
So we had heard, Ben had specifically heard about how great Steve was in Bruce Almighty,

705
00:30:07.570 --> 00:30:13.950
and he really had that amazing sort of scene-stealing moment where he's speaking in tongues as the newscaster.

706
00:30:14.070 --> 00:30:14.350
Yes.

707
00:30:14.350 --> 00:30:20.750
And everybody knew he was, you know, really great, and it would be a good choice for this.

708
00:30:21.850 --> 00:30:27.030
And I'm very methodical, as you know, and I was like, okay, that sounds great.

709
00:30:27.590 --> 00:30:36.230
First, I want to get the casting director on board, and I, you know, really wanted to use Allison Jones from her works on Freaks and Geeks, which I loved.

710
00:30:37.170 --> 00:30:40.890
And it took two weeks to get her on board, and in those two weeks, Steve took this other show.

711
00:30:41.850 --> 00:30:43.610
And we were like, ah, you know.

712
00:30:43.610 --> 00:30:44.470
I didn't know that.

713
00:30:44.690 --> 00:30:45.450
Yeah, it was awful.

714
00:30:45.730 --> 00:30:56.210
So then we went on this incredibly long process of looking at every single person in town who could possibly be in the age range for Michael Scott,

715
00:30:56.490 --> 00:31:01.030
and including various writer friends that I had who I threw into the mix.

716
00:31:01.030 --> 00:31:05.250
And, you know, just normal people, like I'd be walking down the street and go, oh, you look good.

717
00:31:07.270 --> 00:31:08.110
Read this.

718
00:31:08.290 --> 00:31:08.470
Yeah.

719
00:31:08.750 --> 00:31:10.570
But anyway, it came full circle.

720
00:31:10.770 --> 00:31:17.650
And then after three months of intense casting, we got a little tip-off from Kevin Riley that maybe come to Papa wasn't going to go the distance.

721
00:31:17.650 --> 00:31:22.530
And we could get Steve in second position to that other show, and we'd be safe.

722
00:31:22.730 --> 00:31:23.050
And so.

723
00:31:23.510 --> 00:31:25.110
Let's get into Booze Cruise.

724
00:31:25.330 --> 00:31:25.670
Oh, my gosh.

725
00:31:25.870 --> 00:31:25.990
All right.

726
00:31:25.990 --> 00:31:26.130
All right.

727
00:31:26.390 --> 00:31:26.670
No card.

728
00:31:26.670 --> 00:31:27.050
Talk to you all day.

729
00:31:27.290 --> 00:31:27.450
No card.

730
00:31:27.610 --> 00:31:28.170
Okay, okay.

731
00:31:28.410 --> 00:31:29.670
I have a cold open.

732
00:31:29.950 --> 00:31:30.270
No card.

733
00:31:30.350 --> 00:31:30.690
Oh, wait.

734
00:31:30.710 --> 00:31:31.650
Greg has a no card.

735
00:31:31.710 --> 00:31:31.890
Greg has one.

736
00:31:31.970 --> 00:31:32.130
Go.

737
00:31:32.230 --> 00:31:32.370
No card.

738
00:31:32.510 --> 00:31:33.150
Minute 45.

739
00:31:33.610 --> 00:31:33.970
Oh.

740
00:31:34.270 --> 00:31:34.630
Yes.

741
00:31:34.750 --> 00:31:34.990
Yes.

742
00:31:35.210 --> 00:31:35.260
Yes.

743
00:31:35.260 --> 00:31:35.830
An early one.

744
00:31:35.830 --> 00:31:38.610
I believe Kevin is packing a Speedo.

745
00:31:38.770 --> 00:31:40.530
A metallic blue Speedo.

746
00:31:40.530 --> 00:31:41.010
Yes.

747
00:31:41.330 --> 00:31:42.630
And lots of condoMs.

748
00:31:42.650 --> 00:31:43.570
And condoms, yes.

749
00:31:43.890 --> 00:31:45.250
And lots of condoMs.

750
00:31:45.910 --> 00:31:52.410
Also, we have a great Jim Pam talking head, a joint talking head, which I know was really fun for you guys to do right before that.

751
00:31:52.570 --> 00:31:52.810
Yes.

752
00:31:52.810 --> 00:31:58.930
Where we're reading the memo from Michael that he has sent around telling us to prepare for a surprise outing.

753
00:31:59.150 --> 00:32:05.110
He tells us to pack a toothbrush, a swimsuit, a ski mask, and wear your rubber-soled shoes.

754
00:32:05.110 --> 00:32:08.530
I love how delighted Pam is by Jim and this talking head.

755
00:32:08.650 --> 00:32:17.690
And also, at 1 minute 53 seconds, please notice the winter Scranton backdrop outside of the window of our talking head.

756
00:32:17.990 --> 00:32:20.350
We would change those backdrops seasonally.

