And she remembers thinking that if NBC knew that their star was just, didn't have a life

1506
01:03:28.470 --> 01:03:32.950
jacket and was, you know, being pulled off back to the harbor, they would be upset.

1507
01:03:32.950 --> 01:03:41.010
And then she got on the boat and she thought it was Oscar, Brian, and somebody from the

1508
01:03:41.010 --> 01:03:41.930
wardrobe department.

1509
01:03:42.830 --> 01:03:44.670
I wrote about it the day of.

1510
01:03:44.670 --> 01:03:44.950
But that was you.

1511
01:03:45.210 --> 01:03:45.350
Yeah.

1512
01:03:45.710 --> 01:03:47.530
Oh, she says, I'm not sure if Angela was there or not.

1513
01:03:47.890 --> 01:03:50.650
And then she talks, everything was fine pulling away.

1514
01:03:50.810 --> 01:03:53.830
We were enjoying the smell of the ocean, the lights in the distance.

1515
01:03:53.970 --> 01:03:56.030
And then all of a sudden, nothing.

1516
01:03:56.190 --> 01:03:57.030
The engine died.

1517
01:03:57.650 --> 01:03:58.630
So you were there.

1518
01:03:58.630 --> 01:04:03.830
So this is what I wrote the day after we filmed the scene.

1519
01:04:04.170 --> 01:04:04.630
All right.

1520
01:04:04.750 --> 01:04:08.190
I wrote, one of the coolest things that happened to me while we shot this episode happened the

1521
01:04:08.190 --> 01:04:10.870
last night, or I guess I should say the morning of.

1522
01:04:11.010 --> 01:04:14.490
Rain, Phyllis, and Brian and I were all wrapped at 5.30 a.m.

1523
01:04:14.870 --> 01:04:19.430
We were on the main boat, but they said they had more shooting to do so we could stay on

1524
01:04:19.430 --> 01:04:21.910
the big boat and like sleep in a booth.

1525
01:04:22.490 --> 01:04:22.730
Yeah.

1526
01:04:22.970 --> 01:04:27.190
Or they said they had these little rubber boats that would pull up next to the big boat that

1527
01:04:27.190 --> 01:04:29.110
we could get on and they would take us to shore.

1528
01:04:29.730 --> 01:04:31.470
So we said, okay, we'll do that.

1529
01:04:31.570 --> 01:04:34.730
They said it's about a 15-minute boat ride on the little rubber dinghy.

1530
01:04:35.650 --> 01:04:36.830
So we decided to do it.

1531
01:04:36.950 --> 01:04:39.090
It was really tricky getting on the rubber boat.

1532
01:04:39.230 --> 01:04:39.530
Yeah.

1533
01:04:39.730 --> 01:04:42.870
We just had to climb out a ladder off the side of the boat.

1534
01:04:42.990 --> 01:04:43.910
And these guys came.

1535
01:04:43.970 --> 01:04:44.530
It was very unstable.

1536
01:04:44.750 --> 01:04:44.970
Very.

1537
01:04:45.070 --> 01:04:47.210
Phyllis said that at one point she had to switch boats.

1538
01:04:47.450 --> 01:04:48.870
She was terrified of falling in.

1539
01:04:48.990 --> 01:04:49.330
Yes.

1540
01:04:49.330 --> 01:04:55.870
And I said, okay, the little rubber boat, picture the kind of like rubber boat that the

1541
01:04:55.870 --> 01:05:00.550
Navy SEALs do the backflips off of with the little like me, like outboat.

1542
01:05:00.670 --> 01:05:01.570
That's what this looked like.

1543
01:05:01.870 --> 01:05:08.730
So we're lowered down into it and it was me, Rain, Jen, one of our wardrobe gals, Phyllis

1544
01:05:08.730 --> 01:05:09.250
and Brian.

1545
01:05:09.530 --> 01:05:11.410
It was tiny and really bouncy.

1546
01:05:11.590 --> 01:05:12.150
We took off.

1547
01:05:12.250 --> 01:05:14.970
It was dark out and the ocean looked like black glass.

1548
01:05:15.170 --> 01:05:16.310
It was very peaceful.

1549
01:05:16.690 --> 01:05:17.810
Oh, it's so poetic.

1550
01:05:17.810 --> 01:05:18.770
Yeah, sorry.

1551
01:05:19.390 --> 01:05:24.570
About five minutes into our ride, our engine made a weird worrying sound and stopped working.

1552
01:05:25.250 --> 01:05:29.170
And then we heard the boat driver radio and say, the engine's dead.

1553
01:05:29.290 --> 01:05:30.430
You need to come get us.

1554
01:05:30.730 --> 01:05:33.710
Brian started laughing and said, you've got to be kidding me.

1555
01:05:34.410 --> 01:05:38.050
Then we just floated in the darkness, which felt like for a while.

1556
01:05:38.730 --> 01:05:40.970
It was quiet and peaceful.

1557
01:05:41.170 --> 01:05:42.230
It turned into the Titanic.

1558
01:05:42.510 --> 01:05:42.990
I know.

1559
01:05:43.230 --> 01:05:43.830
I know.

1560
01:05:44.010 --> 01:05:47.710
And then we, another rubber boat pulled up and then we had to just

1561
01:05:47.710 --> 01:05:52.390
straddle from one rubber boat with just two guys kind of holding it together into the

1562
01:05:52.390 --> 01:05:52.830
other one.

1563
01:05:52.950 --> 01:05:54.090
This was very tricky.

1564
01:05:54.650 --> 01:05:56.870
We slowly made our way past the Queen Mary.

1565
01:05:57.070 --> 01:05:59.310
Picture an old cruise ship that looks like the Titanic.

1566
01:05:59.610 --> 01:06:04.310
And there was a huge plume of fire coming from a refinery behind it.

1567
01:06:04.710 --> 01:06:06.710
Rain said to me, this is so surreal.

1568
01:06:07.250 --> 01:06:08.990
And that's exactly what it was.

1569
01:06:08.990 --> 01:06:12.350
You know, the wheels really came off when we went on locations.

1570
01:06:12.690 --> 01:06:14.390
We talk about this a little bit.

1571
01:06:14.730 --> 01:06:19.310
I mean, you know, this is for season nine, but you know, death bus almost killed us all.

1572
01:06:19.730 --> 01:06:19.990
Yes.

1573
01:06:20.130 --> 01:06:20.650
Work bus.

1574
01:06:20.750 --> 01:06:21.010
Work bus.

1575
01:06:21.010 --> 01:06:21.490
Work bus.

1576
01:06:22.010 --> 01:06:24.070
We've called it death bus for so long.

1577
01:06:24.230 --> 01:06:25.630
The cast calls it death bus.

1578
01:06:25.870 --> 01:06:26.390
All right.

1579
01:06:26.470 --> 01:06:27.870
Well, we'll take a break and we'll be back.

1580
01:06:28.070 --> 01:06:36.930
All right.

1581
01:06:36.950 --> 01:06:38.110
So we are back from break.

1582
01:06:38.810 --> 01:06:40.750
I have a question for you, Greg.

1583
01:06:40.910 --> 01:06:41.050
Yeah.

1584
01:06:41.190 --> 01:06:42.830
I've been waiting to ask you this.

1585
01:06:42.830 --> 01:06:47.310
I know we're sort of jumping around with time codes, but at 11 minutes, 34 seconds,

1586
01:06:47.610 --> 01:06:53.070
there's this beautiful shot that goes outside of the boat and goes around the boat.

1587
01:06:53.230 --> 01:06:55.010
You pass Dwight, right?

1588
01:06:55.210 --> 01:06:57.590
Like steering and it goes around the boat.

1589
01:06:57.750 --> 01:06:58.550
How did we do that?

1590
01:06:59.030 --> 01:07:00.390
That is Kent Zabornak.

1591
01:07:00.790 --> 01:07:05.490
So Kent was our line producer and he was also in the DGA.

1592
01:07:06.670 --> 01:07:12.170
And, you know, he managed to launch us on the budget that we had, which was tiny.

1593
01:07:12.830 --> 01:07:17.970
You know, into Long Beach Harbor on a boat for days and get hotel rooms for everybody.

1594
01:07:18.910 --> 01:07:22.950
And he realized that we really needed a shot like that.

1595
01:07:23.010 --> 01:07:30.490
And he shot that himself with his own equipment, you know, while we were shooting the rest of it.

1596
01:07:31.730 --> 01:07:36.610
Because he just had an instinct that there was no way the production could get that and stop everything.

1597
01:07:36.910 --> 01:07:41.210
And, you know, so that was kind of a guerrilla move and super valuable.

1598
01:07:41.890 --> 01:07:45.250
And Kent was, you know, fantastic at doing those kind of things.

1599
01:07:45.450 --> 01:07:50.790
Like he was always stitching together amazing stuff on our little budget.

1600
01:07:51.370 --> 01:07:52.370
That is so cool.

1601
01:07:52.530 --> 01:07:53.250
I did not know that.

1602
01:07:53.350 --> 01:07:53.550
Did you?

1603
01:07:53.790 --> 01:07:53.910
No.

1604
01:07:53.910 --> 01:07:59.590
Like when I saw it, I was like, wait, I know Randall and Matt were inside with us.

1605
01:07:59.630 --> 01:08:00.270
Yeah, who did that, right?

1606
01:08:00.270 --> 01:08:00.930
Our camera guys.

1607
01:08:00.930 --> 01:08:02.070
I was like, how did that happen?

1608
01:08:02.070 --> 01:08:04.130
And then I was like, is that a plane?

1609
01:08:04.130 --> 01:08:05.990
Like, it's in the air.

1610
01:08:06.210 --> 01:08:08.070
Like how that, I mean, is it a drone?

1611
01:08:08.230 --> 01:08:08.970
Like, what is it?

1612
01:08:08.990 --> 01:08:10.130
He's on one of those dinghies.

1613
01:08:10.470 --> 01:08:12.230
He's on one of those Zodiac dinghies.

1614
01:08:12.490 --> 01:08:12.850
Okay.

1615
01:08:13.250 --> 01:08:13.430
Yeah.

1616
01:08:13.690 --> 01:08:14.030
All right.

1617
01:08:14.190 --> 01:08:14.470
Wow.

1618
01:08:14.690 --> 01:08:18.310
This is another question about a camera movement.

1619
01:08:19.110 --> 01:08:29.530
So, you know, after they're at the bar and they're all talking about who they would save, Jim gets up his courage and he's going to walk over to Pam and he says,

1620
01:08:29.710 --> 01:08:31.090
I would save the receptioniSt.

1621
01:08:31.190 --> 01:08:32.510
I think I need to make that clear.

1622
01:08:32.510 --> 01:08:37.190
Well, right before that, Greg Phelps noticed at 14 minutes, 37 seconds,

1623
01:08:37.410 --> 01:08:43.530
there's a continuous shot of Jim looking down the length of the boat to Pam as he thinks about who he would save.

1624
01:08:43.870 --> 01:08:49.010
The camera then zooms in on Pam at the exact moment she has a really cute laughing smile.

1625
01:08:49.410 --> 01:08:51.450
How did you time that out so perfectly?

1626
01:08:52.230 --> 01:08:56.550
He wants to know, Jenna, were you given a cue when the camera was about to zoom in on you?

1627
01:08:56.650 --> 01:08:58.750
But we used to do that stuff all the time.

1628
01:08:59.790 --> 01:09:00.970
That was, go ahead.

1629
01:09:00.970 --> 01:09:12.970
People, I think, have the wrong idea about how we did the show in a way because like most filmmaking is super intentional and you would say to the camera operator, okay,

1630
01:09:13.310 --> 01:09:19.570
at this signal you need to zoom in and then you'd try and get the actor to do it at exactly the right time.

1631
01:09:19.570 --> 01:09:37.370
And our show was more like the theory of natural selection kind of like there would be a situation and I would give notes or the directors would give notes to the camera operators that were not about what they did specifically but more about their intentions.

1632
01:09:37.370 --> 01:09:45.150
So we would say, okay, you're really interested in what he sees down at the end of the hall, so just go find it.

1633
01:09:45.330 --> 01:09:51.950
And we had all these people who'd come from reality shows who were used to finding the story on their own.

1634
01:09:53.070 --> 01:09:55.750
And that turned out great, you know.

1635
01:09:55.950 --> 01:09:58.870
And I can't really speak for Ken as the director.

1636
01:09:58.870 --> 01:10:04.290
He directed it and he might have known exactly what he was going for and kept doing it until he got it.

1637
01:10:04.510 --> 01:10:17.350
But a lot of the times there were just lots of happy accidents and then you'd tell the story through all the happy accidents and you had enough like flexibility that you could have gone a bunch of different ways with it.

1638
01:10:17.350 --> 01:10:19.650
I think our editors don't get enough credit.

1639
01:10:19.850 --> 01:10:25.950
Well, first of all, I think our camera operators don't get enough credit for all of those happy accidents.

1640
01:10:26.070 --> 01:10:33.030
But then you would assemble those with the editors in the editing room to tell even more of a story than what was in the script.

1641
01:10:34.270 --> 01:10:34.750
Totally.

1642
01:10:34.910 --> 01:10:36.390
The editors are amazing.

1643
01:10:36.670 --> 01:10:38.070
We had fantastic editors.

1644
01:10:38.470 --> 01:10:39.330
This one was Dean.

1645
01:10:40.410 --> 01:10:43.370
Dave Rogers, as you know, I'm still working with.

1646
01:10:43.490 --> 01:10:44.970
Work with him all the time.

1647
01:10:45.170 --> 01:10:45.430
Brilliant.

1648
01:10:45.430 --> 01:10:48.030
I mean, Claire Scanlon.

1649
01:10:48.030 --> 01:10:48.810
Claire Scanlon.

1650
01:10:49.010 --> 01:10:49.630
Also brilliant.

1651
01:10:51.550 --> 01:11:04.610
And there was enormous amounts of leeway for the editors and in post because like a documentary, we shot way more stuff than we could ever possibly use.

1652
01:11:04.650 --> 01:11:13.050
And so our rough cuts were always about 38 minutes long, but the show's only 21 minutes, you know, six seconds or something like that.

1653
01:11:13.050 --> 01:11:16.470
And so tons of good material was discarded.

1654
01:11:17.850 --> 01:11:20.990
And that was like a large part of my job.

1655
01:11:21.130 --> 01:11:25.650
Like I loved being on set, you know, and I also loved being with the writers.

1656
01:11:25.930 --> 01:11:34.730
But this whole other area of my job was sitting with the editors and, you know, and they're fun people too with great, you know, creative ideas.

1657
01:11:34.730 --> 01:11:40.990
And you could tell so many different versions of the show by what you left in and what you took out.

1658
01:11:41.450 --> 01:11:49.430
And like there was – and there was always a thing where you'd like – you'd go – everybody kind of agreed on the first six or eight minutes to get rid of, you know.

1659
01:11:49.610 --> 01:11:57.310
And like it probably – we all agreed like having Oscar make snarking mean comments is not great, you know, or whatever it is.

1660
01:11:57.310 --> 01:12:01.190
But then you'd get to like about 26 minutes and there was a lot of really good stuff.

1661
01:12:01.190 --> 01:12:06.790
And I used to have all these regrets about things that were great that couldn't get in the show.

1662
01:12:06.950 --> 01:12:14.610
And one of them for this, if you remember, was Creed sitting in with the band and us finding out that he'd been in the grassroots.

1663
01:12:14.930 --> 01:12:15.250
Yeah.

1664
01:12:15.250 --> 01:12:17.690
And that was like fantastic.

1665
01:12:17.690 --> 01:12:30.370
And we had all this footage and I found footage of him from, you know, late 60s and, you know, playing guitar with the grassroots on Playboy After Dark with Hugh Hefner.

1666
01:12:30.670 --> 01:12:40.510
Like all this great footage, which was going to be the B-roll under the, you know, under him talking about playing with all these greats in the late 60s.

1667
01:12:40.550 --> 01:12:41.910
And that was in for a really long time.

1668
01:12:42.010 --> 01:12:43.490
I think it's in the DVD extras.

1669
01:12:43.770 --> 01:12:44.110
It is.

1670
01:12:44.170 --> 01:12:45.130
It's in the deleted scenes.

1671
01:12:45.250 --> 01:12:45.770
It's great.

1672
01:12:46.070 --> 01:12:51.710
Michael, the guitar player is going to take a break and then Michael tries to fill in for him and he's horrible.

1673
01:12:51.970 --> 01:12:53.950
And then Creed says, let me give it a shot.

1674
01:12:54.110 --> 01:12:56.870
And then he just gets up there and rocks out.

1675
01:12:56.970 --> 01:12:58.970
And it's so cool and satisfying.

1676
01:12:59.130 --> 01:13:03.170
And then he has a talking head where he explains that he is himself, basically.

1677
01:13:03.430 --> 01:13:03.770
Yeah.

1678
01:13:03.770 --> 01:13:06.390
He is Creed Breton, member of Grassroots.

1679
01:13:06.670 --> 01:13:06.810
Yeah.

1680
01:13:07.010 --> 01:13:09.350
And that he kind of fried his brain with drug use.

1681
01:13:09.490 --> 01:13:09.630
Yeah.

1682
01:13:09.630 --> 01:13:12.190
And now he can barely concentrate on his job.

1683
01:13:12.870 --> 01:13:16.910
And then there's a talking head of Angela going, he wasn't that good or something like that.

1684
01:13:18.070 --> 01:13:18.850
I've seen better.

1685
01:13:19.030 --> 01:13:19.170
Yeah.

1686
01:13:19.510 --> 01:13:20.150
Oh, man.

1687
01:13:20.350 --> 01:13:23.790
By the way, I would wear a shirt that says, I'd save the receptioniSt.

1688
01:13:23.990 --> 01:13:24.470
I think that is.

1689
01:13:24.470 --> 01:13:27.490
We should have shirts that say, I'd save the receptioniSt.

1690
01:13:27.530 --> 01:13:28.430
I think that's so cute.

1691
01:13:28.550 --> 01:13:32.710
I think Ken did a really good job with that, too, because that was, I believe, supposed to just be a talking head.

1692
01:13:33.610 --> 01:13:33.930
Yes.

1693
01:13:33.970 --> 01:13:35.530
In the script, it's a talking head.

1694
01:13:35.530 --> 01:13:35.690
Yeah.

1695
01:13:35.970 --> 01:13:40.670
And he really rightly said, well, where exactly did he say this?

1696
01:13:40.750 --> 01:13:49.250
Because he had the thought, and then he walks over to Pam, and then she gets interrupted by Roy setting a date.

1697
01:13:49.310 --> 01:13:54.290
So he couldn't figure out where there was going to be a time to interview him about this.

1698
01:13:54.290 --> 01:14:02.430
And so he sort of invented this on-the-fly interview where he kind of just passed the cameraman and set it into camera, and then it was cool.

1699
01:14:02.670 --> 01:14:02.910
Yeah.

1700
01:14:02.970 --> 01:14:03.490
Very cool.

1701
01:14:03.510 --> 01:14:04.150
I really liked that.

1702
01:14:04.330 --> 01:14:05.450
No, and you're with him.

1703
01:14:05.630 --> 01:14:06.730
Like, Jim's got his courage.

1704
01:14:06.790 --> 01:14:08.510
He's going to save the receptioniSt.

1705
01:14:08.710 --> 01:14:16.370
He almost gets up to Pam, and that's when Roy stands up and says that he's setting a wedding date, June 10th.

1706
01:14:16.370 --> 01:14:24.550
A fan question from Ed's Arquiza said, was Pam genuinely happy when Roy proposed, or was she just caught up in the moment?

1707
01:14:24.830 --> 01:14:26.170
Well, I looked at the script.

1708
01:14:26.430 --> 01:14:28.970
The script said, Pam looks happy.

1709
01:14:29.230 --> 01:14:35.330
So I played it that Pam was genuinely kind of overcome with this moment.

1710
01:14:35.650 --> 01:14:37.930
You know, she's been trying to get Roy's attention.

1711
01:14:37.930 --> 01:14:42.010
I think she wants him to treat her like this so badly.

1712
01:14:42.790 --> 01:14:43.930
And so...

1713
01:14:43.930 --> 01:14:46.350
She's also invested years into this relationship.

1714
01:14:46.790 --> 01:14:46.990
I know.

1715
01:14:46.990 --> 01:14:52.110
And I feel for her because she's like, all right, we're going to do it.

1716
01:14:52.210 --> 01:14:52.890
This is it.

1717
01:14:53.090 --> 01:14:53.290
Yeah.

1718
01:14:53.450 --> 01:14:57.630
I think it makes it so much worse, too, because he had his moment on the deck.

1719
01:14:57.770 --> 01:14:58.090
Yeah.

1720
01:14:58.310 --> 01:15:01.570
And she was quite receptive there, and then he didn't go for anything.

1721
01:15:01.890 --> 01:15:08.450
And then here, look how happy she is that she, you know, finally, after three years of being, you know, strung along.

1722
01:15:08.950 --> 01:15:09.190
Yeah.

1723
01:15:09.410 --> 01:15:15.850
And then you could just picture like, oh, maybe I could have made that face on her if I'd said something upstairs.

1724
01:15:15.850 --> 01:15:16.230
Yes.

1725
01:15:43.030 --> 01:15:45.830
You know?

1726
01:15:46.230 --> 01:15:47.270
That's not a real name.

1727
01:15:47.350 --> 01:15:47.610
Come on.

1728
01:15:47.650 --> 01:15:48.390
First, it's not real.

1729
01:15:48.570 --> 01:15:50.210
Also, you just rolled into it.

1730
01:15:50.350 --> 01:15:51.530
Like, I thought it was part of your sentence.

1731
01:15:51.670 --> 01:15:53.310
You're like, and then Katie, Jibbity Jenkins.

1732
01:15:53.530 --> 01:15:53.770
Yeah.

1733
01:15:53.870 --> 01:15:55.130
I was like, is this a poor...

1734
01:15:55.130 --> 01:15:56.490
That's like a Missouri thing.

1735
01:15:56.770 --> 01:15:57.790
I'll be Jibbity Jenkins.

1736
01:15:57.970 --> 01:15:58.890
From Dogpatch.

1737
01:15:59.090 --> 01:15:59.730
Jibbity Jenkins.

